            <ArticleTitle>Sub-anesthetic doses of bupivacaine or lidocaine increase peripheral ICS-205 930-induced analgesia against inflammatory pain in rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>39-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intraplantar co-administration of sub-anesthetic doses of bupivacaine (2.5 microg) or lidocaine (7.5 microg) increased the dose- and time-dependent analgesic effects of the 5-HT3 receptor antagonist, 3-a-tropanyl-1-H-indole-3-carboxylic ester (ICS-205 930) (1-100 microg; 50 microl) against inflammatory pain induced by hindpaw inoculation with complete Freund's adjuvant. The effects of bupivacaine were greater than lidocaine at all doses of ICS-205 930 tested. These findings may reflect facilitation of ICS-205 930 effects through negative allosteric modulation by bupivacaine and lidocaine of peripheral 5-HT3 receptors involved in nociceptive processing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
